>
  <h2>What the research says</h2>
  <p>Franco-Garcia and colleagues measured how each shoulder moved during the bench press in 27 people, using wearable motion sensors, at loads from 55 percent up to 100 percent of their one rep max. They found that:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The left shoulder had a larger range of motion than the right at every load.</li>
    <li>When lowering the bar at 85 to 100 percent of max, speed and acceleration differed between the two shoulders.</li>
    <li>When pressing up, the two shoulders mostly matched, apart from one speed measure in the middle load range.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The takeaway is that the two sides of a bench press are rarely identical, even in people who are not complaining about it. We did not find a study showing that correcting a difference raises the bench press max or lowers injury risk, so what follows is practical coaching, and it is labelled that way.</p>

  <h2>First, check whether it is real</h2>
  <p>These checks are general coaching practice, not results from a study.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Film the set.</strong> Put the camera behind your head or at the foot of the bench, at bar height. Pause the video at the chest and at lockout. Is one end of the bar higher at both points, or only late in the set when you are tired?</li>
    <li><strong>Test each arm alone.</strong> Do a single-arm dumbbell press for a hard set of the same number of reps on each side, with the same range of motion. Write down both weights.</li>
    <li><strong>Check your setup.</strong> Use the marks on the bar to place your hands the same distance from the center. Set your eyes under the bar, your feet and hips evenly, and both shoulder blades pulled back the same amount. Unrack the same way every time.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A tilt that shows up on every rep from the first one points to setup or strength. A tilt that only appears on the last grinding reps points more to fatigue.</p>

  <h2>Common reasons the bar tilts</h2>
  <p>This is a list coaches check, not a study result.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>One side is stronger than the other.</li>
    <li>The hands are not centered on the bar.</li>
    <li>The shoulder blades, hips or feet are set unevenly.</li>
    <li>Fatigue late in the set, with the weaker side giving out first.</li>
    <li>A past injury or tightness on one side.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What to do about it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Fix the setup first.</strong> It costs nothing and removes the easy explanations.</li>
    <li><strong>Work the weaker side first.</strong> In single-arm work, start with the weaker arm and do the same number of reps on the stronger side. Never let the strong arm do extra.</li>
    <li><strong>Slow the reps down.</strong> Jordan's first move when a lift stops progressing is to slow the lowering phase or pause at the bottom for 3 seconds. Slow, paused reps also make a side that drifts easier to see and feel.</li>
    <li><strong>Retest in six to eight weeks</strong> with the same test, and keep the result only if it changed.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>When to get it looked at</h2>
  <p>If the difference comes with pain, numbness or tingling, a sudden change, or follows an injury, see a doctor or physical therapist before training through it. A coach cannot tell you what is going on inside a joint.</p>

  <h2>Do not chase perfect symmetry</h2>
  <p>The study above found differences between shoulders in people who were lifting without any stated problem. A small difference that does not change your bar path or bother you is not a problem to fix. Spend your effort on a gap you can see on video or feel when you press.</p>
